---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Icon from '../../components/Icon.astro';
import PullQuote from '../../components/PullQuote.astro';
import { getGitDates, formatDate } from '../../lib/git-dates';

// New page, nicotine pouches getting their own section (2026-10-02).
// Sourcing: the JAMA 2026 review (Piasecki et al., src/content/research/
// 2026-nicotine-pouches.md) and the 2026 scoping review (Travis et al.,
// .../2026-oral-nicotine-pouches-and-public-health-evidence-from-an.md),
// both already in the science library — linked, not re-summarised from
// scratch. India SLT toll figures match /india/smokeless-tobacco/'s own
// sourcing exactly. No brand named anywhere, including in the FDA link's
// anchor text — the URL itself is the FDA's, not AVI's wording.
const pageDates = getGitDates('src/pages/nicotine-pouches/safety.astro');

const SHORT_ANSWER = "Yes. Pouches carry far less risk than smoking because nothing is burned, and far less than gutka or khaini because they contain no tobacco leaf. In 2026 the US FDA allowed some pouches to be marketed as lowering the risk of mouth cancer, heart disease, lung cancer and stroke compared with cigarettes. They still contain addictive nicotine and can irritate the gums.";

const articleLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Article',
  headline: 'Are Nicotine Pouches Safer Than Smoking or Gutka?',
  datePublished: pageDates.published,
  dateModified: pageDates.modified,
  author: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'Association of Vapers India' },
};

const faqLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: [{
    '@type': 'Question',
    name: 'Are nicotine pouches safer than smoking or gutka?',
    acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: SHORT_ANSWER },
  }],
};
---
<Base
  title="Are Nicotine Pouches Safer Than Smoking or Gutka?"
  rawTitle={true}
  description="Yes — pouches carry far less risk than smoking or chewing tobacco, per the FDA's own review. What the evidence shows, what Sweden's experience demonstrates, and what isn't known yet."
  image="/og/india-law.png"
  wide={true}
>
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(articleLd)} />
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(faqLd)} />

  <section class="breakout safety-hero">
    <div class="breakout-inner">
      <p class="eyebrow">Updated {formatDate(pageDates.modified)}</p>
      <h1>Are nicotine pouches safer than smoking or gutka?</h1>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="safety-body">
    <p class="page-authority">
      By Association of Vapers India · Last reviewed {formatDate(pageDates.modified)}
      <span class="authority-note">· This is AVI's reading of the published evidence, not medical advice.</span>
    </p>
    <p class="short-answer">{SHORT_ANSWER}</p>

    <h2><Icon name="heart" /> Why they're lower risk</h2>
    <p>
      Most of the harm from smoking comes from burning tobacco, not from
      nicotine itself — pouches involve no combustion, so none of the tar
      or thousands of combustion byproducts a cigarette produces. And
      unlike gutka or khaini, a pouch contains no tobacco leaf at all,
      avoiding the leaf-specific carcinogens and abrasive additives
      responsible for most smokeless-tobacco disease. That's the basis for
      the FDA's 2026 order allowing specific lower-risk marketing claims
      against cigarettes, after its own scientific review. <a href="https://www.fda.gov/tobacco-products/ctp-newsroom/fda-authorizes-20-zyn-nicotine-pouches-be-marketed-specific-modified-risk-claim" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read the FDA's announcement →</a>
    </p>

    <h2><Icon name="chart" /> Sweden's record</h2>
    <p>
      Sweden offers the population-scale precedent: a longstanding shift
      from smoking to snus (an oral tobacco product pouches are modelled
      on) produced the <strong>lowest daily smoking rate in Europe
      (~5%)</strong> and 44% lower tobacco-attributable mortality than the
      EU average. <a href="https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC6882181/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read the analysis →</a> Swedish snus is made to different,
      quality-controlled standards than Indian khaini or gutka — the
      lesson is that regulated, lower-risk substitution works at scale,
      not that India's current loose SLT products are already safe.
    </p>

    <h2><Icon name="document" /> India's smokeless tobacco toll</h2>
    <p>
      The product pouches would actually compete with isn't a cigarette —
      it's loose smokeless tobacco. Gutka and khaini are sold openly at
      every paan shop and linked to <strong>350,000+</strong> deaths a
      year, per a <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/25526253/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">peer-reviewed mortality study</a>, among roughly
      <strong>200 million</strong> Indian SLT users (<a href="https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10263025/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GATS-2</a>). Rural gutka
      consumption has <a href="https://theprint.in/economy/indias-deadly-surge-in-tobacco-use-60-urban-spike-in-a-decade-565-gutka-boom-in-rural-households/2870574/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">surged 565% over the past decade</a>. A
      demonstrably lower-risk pouch stays off the market while that stays
      legal. <a href="/india/smokeless-tobacco/">The full harm reduction case →</a>
    </p>

    <h2><Icon name="search" /> What isn't known yet</h2>
    <p>
      The evidence isn't one-sided. A 2026 JAMA review found rising use of
      pouches among teenagers and young people a genuine concern alongside
      their lower relative risk. A separate 2026 scoping review in
      <em>Nicotine &amp; Tobacco Research</em> found pouch sales have
      tripled since 2021 and that most people who try them keep smoking
      cigarettes too, rather than switching completely — so "lower risk
      than smoking" isn't the same as "replaces smoking" for most current
      users. Neither review changes the FDA's underlying chemistry-based
      finding, but both are reasons to regulate rather than ignore this
      category. <a href="/science/2026-nicotine-pouches/">Read the JAMA review →</a> <a href="/science/2026-oral-nicotine-pouches-and-public-health-evidence-from-an/">Read the scoping review →</a>
    </p>

    <PullQuote
      quote="Using [nicotine pouches] instead of cigarettes puts you at a lower risk of mouth cancer, heart disease, lung cancer, stroke, emphysema, and chronic bronchitis."
      attribution="US FDA, modified-risk marketing order, June 2026"
    />

    <h2><Icon name="document" /> Sources</h2>
    <ul class="sources-list">
      <li><a href="https://www.fda.gov/tobacco-products/ctp-newsroom/fda-authorizes-20-zyn-nicotine-pouches-be-marketed-specific-modified-risk-claim" target="_blank" rel="noopener">US FDA, modified-risk marketing order, June 2026</a></li>
      <li><a href="/science/2026-nicotine-pouches/">Piasecki et al., "Nicotine Pouches," JAMA, 2026</a></li>
      <li><a href="/science/2026-oral-nicotine-pouches-and-public-health-evidence-from-an/">Travis et al., "Oral Nicotine Pouches and Public Health: Evidence from an Updated Scoping Review," Nicotine &amp; Tobacco Research, 2026</a></li>
      <li><a href="https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC6882181/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Sweden/snus mortality analysis</a></li>
      <li><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/25526253/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Smokeless-tobacco mortality study (350,000+ deaths/year, India)</a></li>
      <li><a href="https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10263025/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GATS-2, 2016-17</a></li>
      <li><a href="https://theprint.in/economy/indias-deadly-surge-in-tobacco-use-60-urban-spike-in-a-decade-565-gutka-boom-in-rural-households/2870574/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The Print, rural gutka surge, 2026</a></li>
    </ul>
    <p class="callout-note">
      This page describes the published evidence as AVI reads it — not
      medical advice, and not an offer to sell, supply or facilitate
      access to anything.
    </p>

    <div class="safety-related">
      <a href="/nicotine-pouches/" class="related-card">
        <h3>Nicotine pouches in India →</h3>
        <p>The section hub: legal status, safety, and what AVI wants to see change.</p>
      </a>
      <a href="/nicotine-pouches/legal-status/" class="related-card">
        <h3>Are they legal? →</h3>
        <p>No law bans them by name, but there's no legal way to sell one.</p>
      </a>
    </div>
  </div>

  <style>
    .eyebrow {
      font-size: 0.82rem;
      font-weight: 600;
      text-transform: uppercase;
      letter-spacing: 0.08em;
      color: var(--paper);
      margin: 0 0 0.75rem;
    }
    .safety-hero {
      background: var(--green-deep);
      padding: 3.25rem 0 2.75rem;
    }
    .safety-hero h1 { color: #fff; margin: 0; font-size: clamp(1.9rem, 3.6vw, 2.6rem); max-width: 44rem; }

    .safety-body { max-width: var(--max-w); margin: 0 auto; padding: 2.75rem 1.5rem 4rem; }
    .page-authority { font-size: 0.82rem; color: var(--muted); margin: 0 0 1.1rem; }
    .authority-note { display: block; margin-top: 0.2rem; font-style: italic; }
    @media (min-width: 640px) { .authority-note { display: inline; margin-top: 0; } }

    h2 {
      display: flex;
      align-items: center;
      gap: 0.6rem;
      font-size: 1.2rem;
      margin: 2.25rem 0 1rem;
    }
    h2:first-of-type { margin-top: 1.75rem; }
    h2 :global(svg) { width: 1.25rem; height: 1.25rem; color: var(--green); flex-shrink: 0; }

    .sources-list { margin: 0 0 1.5rem; padding-left: 1.2rem; font-size: 0.9rem; line-height: 1.7; }
    .callout-note {
      background: var(--paper-2);
      border-radius: 8px;
      padding: 1rem 1.25rem;
      font-size: 0.88rem;
      font-style: italic;
      color: var(--muted);
      margin: 0 0 2.5rem;
    }

    .safety-related {
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(15rem, 100%), 1fr));
      gap: 1.25rem;
      padding-top: 2rem;
      border-top: 1px solid var(--line);
    }
    .related-card {
      display: block;
      text-decoration: none;
      color: inherit;
      background: var(--paper-2);
      border-radius: 12px;
      padding: 1.25rem 1.4rem;
      transition: transform 0.15s ease;
    }
    .related-card:hover { transform: translateY(-2px); }
    .related-card h3 { margin: 0 0 0.4rem; font-size: 1rem; color: var(--green-deep); }
    .related-card p { margin: 0; font-size: 0.85rem; color: var(--muted); line-height: 1.5; }
  </style>
</Base>
